<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Cleartext, for one thing: pairing with a browser on the same network.

    Android has forbidden plain HTTP by default since API 28, and rightly. Everything this app
    fetches on its own is https: Modland, ASMA, HVSC, The Mod Archive, GitHub. The exception is the
    pairing address, which is a machine on the local network with no certificate and no name a
    certificate could be issued for, and there is no way to say "private addresses only" here: a
    domain-config takes host names, not ranges.

    So the permission is granted broadly and the *choice* is narrowed in code instead:
    `WebRemote.looksLikePairing` refuses a cleartext address unless the host is loopback, a private
    range, or link-local. That is the check that actually protects a playlist from being posted in
    the clear to somewhere public, and it lives where the decision is made rather than in a file
    that can only say yes or no to the whole app.

    When the page is served over https, this can go.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
